.TH SDMEM 3
.SH NAME
sdmem \- block device in RAM, for benchmarking filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind #S /dev

.B /dev/sdZ0/data
.B /dev/sdZ0/ctl
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sdmem
is an
.IR sd (3)
controller whose storage is memory.  It presents one unit, by default at
.BR /dev/sdZ0 ,
holding a disk of 16MB in 512-byte sectors.  Anything that works on a
.IR sd (3)
data file works on it:
.IR mkefs ,
.IR efs (4),
.IR kfs ,
.IR dossrv ,
a raw
.IR dd .
.PP
It exists to separate what a filesystem does from what a medium charges
for it, because on this board those two differ by more than an order of
magnitude and the second hides the first.  Ten file creates onto the SD
card's FAT partition cost 38.8ms each; ten
.IR efs (4)
creates on the same card cost 97.7ms; the same workload on
.I sdmem
costs 28.2ms.  So about seventy of those ninety-seven milliseconds were the
card, and no amount of arithmetic on a single figure separates them as
cleanly as running the same filesystem on a medium that costs nothing.
.PP
.B "The counters are in the driver."
Anything layered on this device is counted, identically, without being
instrumented itself \(em which is the difference between comparing two
filesystems' numbers and comparing two filesystems' instruments.
.PP
The disk is
.IR sparse .
Chunks of 4096 bytes are allocated on first write, so an unwritten disk
costs its chunk table and nothing more, and a read of a chunk never
written returns zeros.  That is also what a newly made disk should look
like to a program that checks whether a region is clear before writing
it;
.I mkefs
does exactly that, and would refuse on uninitialised memory holding
whatever was there before.
.PP
Nothing survives a reboot and nothing tries to.  Do not mount it from a
boot script.
.SS Control
.B /dev/sdZ0/ctl
reports geometry, the modelled latency, how much of the disk is held, and
the counters:
.PP
.EX
geometry 32768 512
sdmem delay 0us chunks 3912 of 4096 (15648KB held)
sdmem reads 17014 ops 136112 secs, writes 4508 ops 36064 secs
sdmem holeread 0 failed 0
.EE
.PP
.I Ops
are transfers \(em one
.I bio
call, whatever its length \(em and
.I secs
are the sectors moved by them.  For a filesystem the first is usually the
interesting one, since a medium charges per operation far more than per
byte.
.I Holeread
counts reads served from a chunk that was never written, and
.I failed
counts transfers refused.
.PP
Writing to
.B ctl
accepts:
.TP
.BI size " nsectors"
Give the disk a new size, dropping whatever it held.  Refused, leaving the
old disk intact, if the chunk table cannot be allocated.  A fully written
disk costs its whole size from the main pool, which on this board is about
50MB in total, so that is the bound that matters rather than any limit
here.  Set it before anything opens
.BR data .
.TP
.BI delay " microseconds"
Charge
.I microseconds
of
.IR microdelay (10)
per transfer, modelling a medium's per-operation cost.  Zero, the default,
charges nothing.
.IP
It is charged ONCE per transfer and not per sector, deliberately: a card's
cost is dominated by the command and not by the bytes, which is why a FAT
create costs tens of milliseconds on a card that streams megabytes a
second.  A per-sector charge would model bandwidth, which measurement on
this board has already shown is not the term that matters.
.IP
Calibration, measured: 200 transfers at
.B "delay 1000"
added 221079us over the same workload at
.BR "delay 0" ,
or 1105us each against 1000 requested.  The overshoot is
.IR microdelay 's
granularity.  Take the counters as exact and the delay as approximate.
.SS Injected faults
The medium is the layer a filesystem's durability argument rests on, and
this device can be told to break.
.PP
Each fault names a TRANSFER NUMBER rather than a probability, because a
test that cannot say which transfer it broke cannot be run twice and get
the same answer \(em and the statement worth making is "survives a loss at
every point in turn", which needs the point to be nameable.  The number is
counted from the last
.BR reset ,
so the usual order is to set the fault and then reset.
.TP
.BI fail " n"
Transfer
.I n
returns an I/O error.  Zero, the default, never.
.TP
.BI failevery " n"
And so does every
.IR n th.
.TP
.BI stop " n"
From transfer
.I n
onward a write is accepted, reported successful, and THROWN AWAY.  Reads
go on working, because the machine has not stopped \(em what has stopped is
the medium keeping anything.
.IP
This is a power cut, and it is the fault worth having.
.B /doc/open-issues-plan.ms
item G1b asks why a superblock became durable while a node it named did
not, and says the test that would settle it is to pull the power, which
needs a human at the board and cannot go in a suite.  This is that test,
repeatable, and it reaches cases a power cut cannot be aimed at: lose the
writes from transfer 40, then from 41, then from 42.
.TP
.BI tear " n"
Transfer
.I n
writes its FIRST SECTOR and reports success.  One sector is the unit a
card guarantees, so a transfer longer than one has no atomicity to rely
on; a format can be designed around that and cannot be if it is never
tested.
.TP
.BI reorder " n"
Hold up to
.I n
writes, at most 64, and apply them BACKWARDS when the queue fills or when
.B flush
is written.  A read sees a held write, because this models a device that
reorders and not one that loses.
.IP
A filesystem whose ordering argument is right survives a reorder inside
one transaction and nothing else, so this is what tells a commit barrier
that is load-bearing from one that is merely believed in.
.TP
.B flush
Apply what is held, backwards, now.
.TP
.B lose
Throw what is held away without applying it \(em which is also what
.B stop
does to the queue when it first fires, because a device that loses new
writes and carefully applies its cache is modelling nothing.
.TP
.B nofault
Forget every fault, losing anything held.
.PP
.B ctl
reports them:
.PP
.EX
sdmem xfer 1832 fail 0 every 0 stop 1200 tear 0 reorder 0 held 0
sdmem injected 0 dropped 632 torn 0 reordered 0
.EE
.PP
.I Injected
counts errors returned on purpose, which is how a test tells the faults it
asked for from the ones it did not;
.I dropped
counts writes accepted and discarded,
.I torn
transfers cut short, and
.I reordered
writes applied out of order.
.SS Control, continued
.TP
.B reset
Zero the counters AND the transfer number the faults are indexed by, which
cannot be separated from them: a workload that does not count from its own
beginning cannot name a transfer.  The faults themselves are a setting,
not a measurement, and are left alone.
.TP
.B zero
Forget the contents, freeing every allocated chunk and leaving the
geometry and the counters alone.  The disk reads as zeros again.
.SH EXAMPLE
Make a filesystem on it, count what three hundred creates cost with no
medium charge, then put an SD card's latency back and see what that buys:
.PP
.EX
disk/mkefs -n 4000 /dev/sdZ0/data
echo reset > /dev/sdZ0/ctl
disk/efs -f /dev/sdZ0/data /n/x
mkdir /n/x/sub
\&... create files in /n/x/sub ...
cat /dev/sdZ0/ctl
echo delay 5000 > /dev/sdZ0/ctl
.EE
.PP
Lose every write from the 400th on \(em a power cut \(em and ask whether the
filesystem still mounts and holds the last state it committed:
.PP
.EX
disk/mkefs -n 4000 /dev/sdZ0/data
echo nofault > /dev/sdZ0/ctl
echo reset > /dev/sdZ0/ctl
echo stop 400 > /dev/sdZ0/ctl
disk/efs -f /dev/sdZ0/data /n/x
\&... write until it stops mattering ...
unmount /n/x
echo nofault > /dev/sdZ0/ctl
disk/efsck /dev/sdZ0/data
.EE
.PP
.B nofault
before
.I efsck
matters: a check run through a device that is still losing writes is
checking the device.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/port/sdmem.c
.PP
.B /tests/fsbench
runs the same workload on
.I efs
and
.I kfs
through this device.  It must run on a board, not under host emu, since
this is a kernel device.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sd (3),
.IR efs (4)
.PP
.B /doc/open-issues-plan.ms
items B1, B1a and F1, which are the measurements this device was built to
stop having to do by hand.
.SH BUGS
A fully written disk is held in the main pool, so sizing one close to the
pool's size will starve everything else before this device reports
anything wrong; the allocation failure surfaces as an I/O error on the
write that could not get a chunk.
.PP
.B size
changes the unit's geometry under
.IR sd (3),
which re-reads it from
.I online
rather than being told.  It bumps the partition version so the change is
noticed, but a size changed while
.B data
is open is not something to rely on.
